// 1. PROFILE: Instruments > Time Profiler (CPU) AND > Allocations.
//    The Allocations template is the one to watch in Swift: ARC retain/release
//    traffic is often invisible on the CPU profile but dominates the runtime.
// 2. BOUND: if the hotspot is 4%, Amdahl caps the win at 4%.
// 3. ONE CHANGE at the measured hotspot. The Swift-specific costs:
//
//    a. ARC traffic: a value type (struct) has no retain/release at all.
struct Point { var x, y: Double }         // no ARC: stored inline
final class CPoint { var x, y: Double }    // each use is a retain/release

//    b. protocol/existential dispatch: an indirect call the optimiser cannot
//       resolve. A generic function over a concrete type is specialised and can
//       be inlined; one over a protocol existential cannot.
func sum<T: Numeric>(_ xs: [T]) -> T { ... }    // specialised: inlinable
func sum(_ xs: [any Numeric]) -> ... { ... }     // existential: boxed, indirect

//    c. bridging to Objective-C: a bridging call crosses the boundary and is
//       not optimisable; keep the hot path in pure Swift.
// 4. VERIFY on a RELEASE build (the debug build optimises differently).
// 5. GUARD: keep a performance test in CI.
